--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NOTE ON CUSTOMISING THESE TERMS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

"All rights reserved" is the most restrictive starting point and is deliberately
chosen as a default, because it is far easier to grant rights later than to claw
them back once granted. Depending on what you want, the usual alternatives are:

  * Open weights, restricted commercial use   -> a source-available licence such
                                                 as the Llama or Gemma community
                                                 licences, or BSL 1.1
  * Open weights, open use                    -> Apache-2.0 or MIT on the weights
  * Research-only release                      -> CC-BY-NC-4.0 on the weights

This file is engineering scaffolding, not legal advice, and I am not a lawyer. If
OXPID is going to carry commercial or funding weight, have a lawyer review the
final wording -- particularly the training-data provenance question, since the
pretraining corpus (NVIDIA Nemotron-ClimbMix) and the SFT mixture (SmolTalk,
MMLU, GSM8K) each carry their own upstream terms that can constrain how the
resulting weights may be licensed.
